From 04d5ba1f0ce1c0edc26a33592c3c32357cdb1df0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oscar <osm1019@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 16:26:58 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dodge: Enable real-time 1080p/4K 120fps and clamp 8K video to
 30fps

Two related video changes that have to travel together, because the second one
only exists to contain the fallout of the first.

Real-time 120fps: the fps allow-list in camera_unit_feature_config.protobuf gains
120 for rear_main, and the app side has to accept it -- yj/o0.smali promotes a
requested rate above 60 to 0x78, com/oplus/camera/module/a.smali reports the
120fps mode as supported, and ia/b.smali stops rejecting 0x78 when it validates
the configured rate.

8K clamp: that allow-list is per-camera with no per-resolution scoping (the
protobuf is a flat string table -- there is no slot to express "120 at 4K but not
at 8K"), so once 120 is picked at 4K the stored fps stays 120 when the user
switches to 8K. The UI correctly hides the option and shows "8K-30", but nothing
rewrites the value, and recording then freezes. 7680x4320 at 120fps needs
15,552,000 MB/s against the core's 7,833,600 maximum, so this is a hardware limit
and not something a config can fix -- 8K caps near 60fps.

Both paths need clamping because they do not share one:
- yj/o0.smali A(II)V (setVideoSize) runs after setVideoFrameRate, so it re-applies
  y(30) once the 8K width (0x1e00) is known. y()/A() dispatch across three
  backends, so clamping in A() covers all of them. This is the mode-switch path.
- ia/i.smali buildSession sends video_30fps when the video size is video_size_8k.
  The mode switch never calls buildSession, and recording never calls A()/y().
